            "explanation": "Describing immediate family in Vietnamese pairs **Kinship Nouns** with strict **Relative Age Distinctions** and regional dialectal terms.\n\n1. **Parents (Cha mẹ):**\n- **bố** [BOH / ɓo˧ˀ˥] (Northern) / **ba** [BAH] (Southern/Central) / **cha** [CHAH] (Formal) — Father\n- **mẹ** [MEH / mɛ˧˨ʔ] (Northern) / **má** [MAH] (Southern) — Mother\n- **cha mẹ** [chah MEH] / **bố mẹ** [boh MEH] — Parents\n\n2. **Siblings (Anh chị em):**\nUnlike English, Vietnamese has no single word for 'brother' or 'sister'. Kinship is defined by **RELATIVE AGE**:\n- **anh** [AHNG / aɲ˧˧] — Older brother -> **anh trai** (specifically male older brother)\n- **chị** [CHEE / ci˧˨ʔ] — Older sister -> **chị gái** (specifically female older sister)\n- **em** [EHM / ɛm˧˧] — Younger sibling (gender-neutral) -> **em trai** (younger brother) / **em gái** (younger sister)\n- **anh chị em** [ahng chee ehm] — Siblings (collective: older brothers, older sisters, and younger siblings!)\n\n3. **Children (Con cái):**\n- **con** [KAWN / kɔn˧˧] — Child\n- **con trai** [kawn CHAI] — Son / Boy\n- **con gái** [kawn GYE] — Daughter / Girl\n- **các con** [kahk KAWN] — The children (plural).",
